|> Exercise
    handle=_1d_blue_yellow_particles_

    Take a system of two particles on the real
    line; at time $t = 0$, the first particle (yellow)
    is at $x = -1$, while the second one (blue)
    is at $x = 1$:

    |> Image
        src=images/1d_blue_yellow_particles.svg

    If the velocity of the yellow particle is set to
    track the position of the blue particle and vice-versa,
    give a qualitative sketch of the
    position-as-a-function-of-time (time on the $x$
    axis, position on the $y$ axis) of the two
    particles. What is the defining geometric relationship
    between the two graphs?

    |> Solution

        At $t = 0$ the blue particle has velocity $-1$,
        which is the position of the yellow particle,
        and the yellow particle has velocity $1$, which
        is the position of the blue particle:

        |> Image
            src=images/1d_blue_yellow_particles_with_velocities.svg

        As we “release” time the two particles thus
        start moving inward towards $0$ and then
        start slowing down, as the opposing particle
        inches closer to $0$. The result is two graphs that
        form a mirror-image radioactive decay:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_three_one_dimensional_particles_blue_yellow_v2.svg

        The “defining geometric relationship” is that
        the slope of one graph equals the value of the
        other graph at any point in time. (One could 
        also name the fact that the graphs have value
        $\pm 1$ at $t = 0$, otherwise the graphs are not
        uniquely determined, as they can be translated
        together left or right by any amount while
        maintaining the former condition about slope.)

        |> SolutionNote

            The sentence

            __a particle whose velocity is minus its
            position
            |> br
            and whose position is $1$ at time $t = 0$__

            is a characterization of the blue particle
            that does not invoke the yellow particle, and
            likewise the sentence

            __a particle whose velocity is minus its
            position
            |> br
            and whose position is $-1$ at time $t = 0$__

            is a characterization of the yellow particle
            that does not invoke the blue particle.

        |> SolutionNote

            The graph of the blue particle is the mirror
            image through the $y$ axis of the graph of the 
            particle of Exercise >>_1d_red_particle_, whose
            velocity is equal to its position and whose
            position is $1$ at $t = 0$:

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch4_three_one_dimensional_particles_red_and_blue_symmetry.svg

            ...the detailed reasoning, as also indicated on the
            figure, being that a mirror symmetry through
            the $y$ axis flips the sign of the slope without
            affecting the value of $y$, so that an equality of
            the form “$y = \te{slope}$” becomes “$y = -\te{slope}$”,
            and vice-versa.
            
            (I.e., the mirror image of the blue
            curve satisfies the constrains of the red curve, and
            vice-versa.)

        !! |> Solution

        !!     For $t > 0$ the yellow and blue particles
        !!     approach $0$ in a kind of “radioactive decay”
        !!     pattern; for $t < 0$,
        !!     they spin
        !!     off to $-\infty$ and $\infty$ respectively at an
        !!     accelerating rate:

        !!     |> Image
        !!         src=images/svg_ch4_three_one_dimensional_particles_blue_yellow.svg

        !!     If we add the graph of the red particle to the
        !!     mix, it is simply the mirror image of the
        !!     blue particle's position through the $y$ axis
        !!     ($y$ axis that is ironically labeled “$x$”):

        !!     |> Image
        !!         src=images/svg_ch4_three_one_dimensional_particles_blue_yellow_red.svg

    !!     Indeed, for the red graph,

    !!     __the slope equals the $y$-value__

    !!     (velocity = position), while

    !!     __the slope equals minus the $y$-value__

    !!     for the blue graph (velocity = position
    !!     of yellow = minus own position).
    !!     (We forgot to mention that the blue and
    !!     yellow graphs are mirror images of one another
    !!     through the horizontal axis—this is one of the
    !!     “symmetries” that the problem statement asks about, though.)
    !!     As taking a mirror image through the $y$ axis
    !!     negates slopes without affecting $y$-coordinates,
    !!     while both the blue and red graphs have the same
    !!     value at $t = 0$, this explains why the mirror
    !!     image of one graph fits the constraints of the other
    !!     and vice-versa:

    !!     |> Image
    !!         src=images/svg_ch4_three_one_dimensional_particles_red_and_blue_symmetry.svg

    !!     Also note that all graphs have slopes of
    !!     $
    !!     \pm 1
    !!     $
    !!     at $t = 0$, as we tried to reflect in the sketches,
    !!     because each corresponding particle position
    !!     is either $1$ or $-1$.
